      "content": "Answer the following question with a single MySQL SQL query against the current database. Explore the semantic network and verify with mysql_query as needed. The SELECT list must contain exactly the columns the question asks for, in the asked order. When the final SQL is verified, call the submit_SQL tool with it.\n\nThe following hints are provided to help you write the SQL:\n\n## Gold Tables\n- ACADEMIC_TERM_PARAMETER\n- ACADEMIC_TERMS\n- COURSE_CATALOG_SUBJECT_OFFERED\n- SIS_DEPARTMENT\n- SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY\n\n## Column Mapping\n- term parameter: ACADEMIC_TERM_PARAMETER.TERM_PARAMETER\n- term code: ACADEMIC_TERM_PARAMETER.TERM_CODE, ACADEMIC_TERMS.TERM_CODE\n- academic year: ACADEMIC_TERMS.ACADEMIC_YEAR\n- department name: COURSE_CATALOG_SUBJECT_OFFERED.DEPARTMENT_NAME, SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_NAME\n- term indicator: ACADEMIC_TERM_PARAMETER.TERM_INDICATOR\n- total units: SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.TOTAL_UNITS\n- enrolled student: SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.NUM_ENROLLED_STUDENTS\n- subjects offered: COURSE_CATALOG_SUBJECT_OFFERED.DEPARTMENT_NAME, SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.OFFER_DEPT_CODE\n- subjects: COURSE_CATALOG_SUBJECT_OFFERED.DEPARTMENT_NAME, SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.OFFER_DEPT_CODE\n- average of total units: SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.TOTAL_UNITS\n- standard deviation of total units: SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.TOTAL_UNITS\n- department: COURSE_CATALOG_SUBJECT_OFFERED.DEPARTMENT_NAME, SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_NAME\n\n## Join Keys\n- ACADEMIC_TERMS.TERM_CODE <-> ACADEMIC_TERM_PARAMETER.TERM_CODE\n- ACADEMIC_TERMS.TERM_CODE <-> COURSE_CATALOG_SUBJECT_OFFERED.EFFECTIVE_TERM_CODE\n- SIS_DEPARTMENT.DEPARTMENT_CODE <-> SUBJECT_OFFERED_SUMMARY.OFFER_DEPT_CODE\n\n## Question\nFor academic term parameters where the term indicator is 'C', show the term parameter, the term code, the academic year, the department name of the offered subject, the running sum of academic years within each department, the average and standard deviation (using STDDEV only and never STDDEV_POP) of the total units for subjects offered by that department (considering only subjects with more than 0 total units and at least 1 enrolled student), and classify the department as 'HIGH_VARIANCE' if its standard deviation (using STDDEV only and never STDDEV_POP) is at least 20% greater than the average, 'LOW_VARIANCE' if its standard deviation (using STDDEV only and never STDDEV_POP) is at least 20% less than the average, or 'AVERAGE_VARIANCE' otherwise. Only include departments that offer at least 3 subjects in those terms. Order the results by department name and academic year. Do not return any rounded answers."
